[%# An application profile rather than a bare `ufw allow`.  setup-ufw-rules
    opens with `ufw reset`, which drops every rule on the guest, and the ufw
    target runs after this one, so a rule added here would be gone by the time
    setup-ufw-rules ran.  Profiles live in /etc/ufw/applications.d, which the
    reset does not touch, so what is declared here is what setup-ufw-rules
    allows -- out as well as in -- once it walks `ufw app list`.  Chrony is
    client-only (port 0 in ntp.chrony.conf.tt), so the outbound half is the
    one this recipe actually needs; the inbound half comes along for free the
    same way it does for every other profile.

    Named ntp-client and not ntp, because ufw refuses a profile whose section
    name is also a service name in /etc/services and says so only as a
    warning on stderr:

        WARN: Skipping .ntp.: also in /etc/services

    -- so the profile would be absent from `ufw app list`, no rule would ever
    be made from it, and chrony would go on reaching nothing without a single
    failure printed anywhere a provision looks. -%]
[ntp-client]
title=NTP client
description=Outbound time sync for chrony
ports=123/udp
